An increasing subsequence means we pick elements in order (left to right) where each element is strictly greater than the previous one. We want to maximize the product of the chosen elements.

This is similar to the Longest Increasing Subsequence (LIS) problem but instead of maximizing length, we maximize the product.

I'll use dynamic programming where `dp[i]` = maximum product of an increasing subsequence ending at index `i`.

For each element `arr[i]`, we look at all previous elements `arr[j]` where `j < i` and `arr[j] < arr[i]`. Then `dp[i] = max(dp[j] * arr[i])` for all valid `j`. If no such `j` exists, `dp[i] = arr[i]` itself.

The answer is the maximum value in the dp array.

